## §8. Registry Design

**[v1.1 — C1 resolved]** **`g_trade_rec_registry` is managed manually by Module 10, not via the frozen `f_push_trade_rec_registry` helper.**

### §8.1 Rationale (C1 resolution)

§3 of v1.0 stated `g_trade_rec_registry` is "appended to exclusively via the frozen `f_push_trade_rec_registry` helper," while §8/§9 of v1.0 required Module 10 to inspect an *evicted* instance's `exitReason` on FIFO overflow to decide whether to emit `EVICTED_OVERFLOW`. These two requirements were mutually incompatible: `f_push_trade_rec_registry`'s final statement calls `f_push_int(g_trade_rec_score, ...)`, so its return type and value are the shifted `int` score (or `na`), not the shifted `TradeRecommendationInstance` — the shifted instance computed internally by the nested `f_push_trade_rec` call is discarded before the wrapper returns.

Per the audit's Option (a), Module 10 resolves this by adopting Module 9's own pattern: `f_m9_push_candidate` does not call an equivalent single-line wrapper for `g_m9_registry` — it manually performs `array.push`/`array.shift` directly, specifically to retain access to the evicted element. Module 10 does the same for `g_trade_rec_registry` and its four parallel arrays.

### §8.2 Mechanism

```
f_m10_push_recommendation(EngineState st, TradeRecommendationInstance inst, string baseId, string action, string rating, int score) =>
    array.push(g_trade_rec_registry, inst)
    array.push(g_trade_rec_base_id, baseId)
    array.push(g_trade_rec_action, action)
    array.push(g_trade_rec_rating, rating)
    array.push(g_trade_rec_score, score)
    TradeRecommendationInstance evicted = na
    if array.size(g_trade_rec_registry) > MAX_TRADE_REC_REGISTRY
        evicted := array.shift(g_trade_rec_registry)
        _ = array.shift(g_trade_rec_base_id)
        _ = array.shift(g_trade_rec_action)
        _ = array.shift(g_trade_rec_rating)
        _ = array.shift(g_trade_rec_score)
    evicted
```

This is called exactly once per accepted candidate (Stage 4), with `score := confidence` (§7, M4 resolution). No second call is ever made for the same instance; all subsequent Stage 5 mutations happen by reference on the object already in the array (§0.11), never via a second push. The five arrays (`g_trade_rec_registry` plus its four parallel arrays) are pushed to and shifted from in lockstep, in the same order, every time — guaranteeing they never drift out of index-sync with one another, the same guarantee `f_m8_push_poi_registry` provides for the three-way POI/extension/dedup set.

### §8.3 Parallel arrays — staleness is accepted, not fixed

Unchanged in substance from v1.0. Per §0.11, this codebase's precedent (`g_poi_state` under Module 8) already establishes that a parallel array snapshotted at creation and never resynced on later mutation is an accepted pattern. Since the manual push in §8.2 is the only write path into these four arrays and it happens only at Stage 4, they will forever reflect creation-time `action`/`rating`/`score` even after Stage 5 sets `exitReason`. This is stated explicitly: **the authoritative live state of any recommendation is always `g_trade_rec_registry` (the object, read by reference), never the parallel arrays, past the instant of creation.** This matches the fact (§0.5) that the given Module 11 dashboard code never reads these parallel arrays at all — they exist for a future consumer (plausibly Module 13's analytics) that is expected to read them as a creation-time snapshot log, not a live-state index.

### §8.4 FIFO overflow

On overflow shift-out of `g_trade_rec_registry` (via `f_m10_push_recommendation`, §8.2), if the evicted instance's `exitReason == ""` (still ACTIVE), emit `EVENT_WARNING`/`"EVICTED_OVERFLOW"` and `AUDIT_WARNING` noting the last known state — identical pattern to Module 9's `f_m9_push_candidate` eviction handling, and now mechanically identical as well (§8.1–§8.2), not just analogous. If the evicted instance is already terminal, no warning is needed (its outcome is already permanently recorded in the event/audit trail from its terminal transition).

### §8.5 Deduplication — new registry, Module 10-local

Unchanged from v1.0:
```
var array<string> g_m10_dedup_keys = array.new<string>()
int MAX_M10_DEDUP_REGISTRY = 500   [DECISION]
```
Tracks `candidate.baseId` values that have been *attempted* (Stage 0, §4.3), independent of Stage 4 success. FIFO-capped at 500. **[DECISION] Why not size this to `MAX_M9_REGISTRY = 100`:** `g_m9_registry` itself cycles at 100, but a `TRIGGERED` candidate can persist there for many bars before eviction; sizing the dedup set any smaller than the realistic attempt-volume over that residency window risks re-processing. 500 is chosen to match `MAX_TRADE_REC_REGISTRY`'s order of magnitude as a reasonable upper bound on lifetime processed-candidate volume, not derived from a hard invariant (this is a discretionary capacity choice, not a correctness requirement below which the system breaks — the same class of choice `MAX_POI_REGISTRY = 50` represents for Module 8).

### §8.6 Replay safety

Unchanged from v1.0. Registry writes happen only inside `barstate.isconfirmed`-gated Stage 0 and Stage 5 blocks (§4), so replaying history from bar 0 reproduces byte-identical registry contents to a live run that reached the same bar — identical to the replay-safety argument Module 9's own document makes for its registry. The manual push/shift mechanism (§8.2) is deterministic index-order `array.push`/`array.shift`, the same primitives `f_m9_push_candidate` already uses under the identical replay-safety argument.

