## §9. Event Bus

**[DECISION] Module 10 events are emitted under `MOD_TR` — the same module ID already used by Module 9 — because no dedicated module ID was reserved for Module 10 anywhere in the frozen Module 1 constants**, unlike every other resource Module 10 needs (UDT, registry, counters, diag fields), all of which *were* pre-reserved. `MOD_TR`'s dashboard display label, `"Trade"`, is the only existing label that semantically fits Trade Recommendation events, and the reasoning Module 9's own document used to justify claiming `MOD_TR` in the first place ("no other module claiming it," §11 of the M9 doc) no longer applies verbatim since Module 9 now claims it — but the evidence instead supports Module 9 and Module 10 *sharing* a single "Trade" domain in the event log, distinguished by `correlationId` prefix (`"M9-"` vs `"M10-"`) and `objectId`, rather than by module ID. This preserves a strictly zero-modification integration with the frozen Module 1 constants file (adding a new module-ID constant was considered and rejected — see §15).

**Events emitted, in the order they can occur within one bar:**

| Stage | Event Type | Severity | Outcome | Trigger |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Stage 1/3 rejection | `EVENT_WARNING` | `SEV_WARNING` | `"REJECTED"` | Any hard/soft gate failure |
| Stage 4 creation | `EVENT_CREATE` | `SEV_INFO` | `"SUCCESS"` | Instance created (via `f_m10_push_recommendation`, §8.2) |
| Stage 5 stop | `EVENT_INVALID` | `SEV_WARNING` | `"SL_HIT"` | Priority-1 transition |
| Stage 5 fulfill | `EVENT_CREATE` | `SEV_INFO` | `"TP_HIT"` | Priority-2 transition |
| Stage 5 expiry | `EVENT_INVALID` | `SEV_WARNING` | `"EXPIRED"` | Priority-3 transition |
| Registry overflow | `EVENT_WARNING` | `SEV_WARNING` | `"EVICTED_OVERFLOW"` | Non-terminal instance FIFO-evicted (§8.4), evaluated using the `evicted` value directly returned by `f_m10_push_recommendation` |

**Ordering within `f_m10_coordinator`:** Stage 0–4 (new-candidate discovery/creation) runs to completion for every newly-observed `TRIGGERED` candidate first, in `g_m9_registry` index order (ascending — deterministic, identical on replay; this is also the order that determines "last processed" for the H4 singleton-diagnostic semantics, §3). Stage 5 (lifecycle evaluation of already-existing instances) runs second, over `g_trade_rec_registry` in index order. A candidate accepted in Stage 4 this bar is excluded from this same bar's Stage 5 pass by the creation-delay guard (§4.4) regardless of which stage would otherwise run first — so there is no ordering ambiguity between a same-bar creation and a same-bar exit for that specific instance.

**Replay-safe ordering:** identical to Module 9's argument — both scans are gated on `barstate.isconfirmed`, iterate arrays in stable index order, and depend on no wall-clock (`timenow`) value.

