## §14. Edge Cases

Unchanged from v1.0, with Edge Case 12 updated to reflect the closed H2 resolution.

1. **Source POI evicted/resolved before Module 10 processes a `TRIGGERED` candidate.** Not a hard gate (§2) — link fields degrade to `"N/A"`/`""`, an `AUDIT_WARNING`-level note is folded into `confidenceFactors`, and the instance is still created (price geometry is self-sufficient on the `TradeCandidate` alone).
2. **Candidate FIFO-evicted from `g_m9_registry` in the same bar it would have triggered.** Module 9's Stage 1 eviction (on overflow push) runs before its own Stage 5-equivalent trigger check within `f_m9_coordinator`; in the rare case the FIFO-oldest evicted entry is the same one about to trigger later that bar, Module 10 never observes the `TRIGGERED` transition at all. This is an inherited limitation of Module 9's frozen 100-slot FIFO registry, not fixable from Module 10 (which has no write access to `g_m9_registry`).
3. **`S.current_context.atr` is `na`.** Only possible inside the `ATR_LENGTH`-bar (14-bar) warm-up window at the very start of chart history — by the time any candidate has traversed Module 9's full multi-stage lifecycle (create → confirm on a later bar → trigger), hundreds of bars have typically elapsed, so this gate is a correctness safeguard rather than a practically-common rejection path. (Carried forward as an acknowledged limitation per M1 — this "practically uncommon" characterization rests on an unstated assumption about `SWING_LOOKBACK`/`ATR_LENGTH` parameter values; not required to be resolved before freeze.)
4. **Degenerate zone (`entryZoneHigh <= entryZoneLow`).** Should never occur given Module 8/9's own invariants; checked defensively per §5 Stage 1.
5. **Confidence below floor but RR healthy, or vice versa.** Independent gates (§4 Stage 3) — either alone is sufficient to reject.
6. **Multiple `TRIGGERED` candidates on the same confirmed bar.** Stage 0 processes each independently, in `g_m9_registry` index order — no shared mutable state between them within the loop, fully deterministic. (See also §3's H4 resolution: this is also the case that determines which candidate's outcome is reflected in the singleton `m10_latest_*` diagnostics.)
7. **Registry overflow (`g_trade_rec_registry` > 500) evicting a still-ACTIVE instance.** Its eventual SL/TP/expiry outcome becomes permanently unknowable — flagged via `EVICTED_OVERFLOW` (§8.4/§9), the same accepted-limitation class as Module 9's own candidate eviction, and now using the same underlying push/shift mechanism (§8.2).
8. **`g_m10_dedup_keys` overflow (>500) evicting an "attempted" marker while the source candidate is still `TRIGGERED` in `g_m9_registry`.** Theoretically permits a duplicate re-attempt at extreme volume. Mirrors the identical, already-accepted risk in `g_poi_dedup_keys` (capped at 50) and `g_m9_dedup_keys` (capped at 100) — not a new risk class introduced by Module 10.
9. **`MOD_TR` shared between Module 9 and Module 10 events.** Any future consumer filtering `g_event_registry` by `module == MOD_TR` expecting only one module's events will get both, distinguishable only by `correlationId` prefix / `objectId` / `description` text, never by the `module` field alone. Documented in §9 as a deliberate, evidence-based reuse decision, not an oversight.
10. **`POIInstance.confluenceScore`/`.grade` permanently `"UNSCORED"`.** Module 10 does not and cannot resolve this (§1, §0.7) — `TradeRecommendationInstance.confidence` is entirely independent.
11. **`poi.mssLink`/`poi.brkLink` always `""`, `poi.liqTier` always `"N/A"` under the frozen Module 8 implementation.** Passed through as-is; Module 10 does not attempt independent MSS/Breaker correlation, since no data-linking infrastructure for that exists in the frozen registries and building one would be inventing a solution the task explicitly disallows.
12. **Dashboard row 63 collision (§11).** **Closed as of v1.1** — formally attributed to Module 11 as an inherited defect, not remediated here, per the H2 resolution in §11.3.
13. **A recommendation resolves (SL/TP/expiry) many bars after creation.** Per the H1 resolution (§3, §4.2), this is architecturally invisible on the dashboard's "latest" block by design — the block is creation-time-only. The resolution is fully visible via `g_trade_rec_registry` (by reference), the Event Bus, and the Audit Bus; it is simply not surfaced in the singleton diagnostics. Not a defect — a stated scope boundary.
14. **More than one `TRIGGERED` candidate processed on the same bar, with differing outcomes (e.g., one accepted, one rejected).** Per the H4 resolution (§3), only the last-processed outcome (in `g_m9_registry` index order) appears in `m10_latest_*`/`m10_reject_reason`. Earlier same-bar outcomes remain fully retrievable via the Event Bus, Audit Bus, and — for accepted candidates — `g_trade_rec_registry`. Not a defect — a stated singleton-snapshot semantic.

